select 'bbbb0001-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus says the definite integral of f from a to b equals…","options":["F(a) - F(b) for any F","F(b) - F(a) where F is an antiderivative of f","the derivative of f at b","f(b) - f(a)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If F'' = f, then the integral of f from a to b is F(b) - F(a). This drives almost every technique in the course."},{"prompt":"The derivative of sin(x) is…","options":["cos(x)","-cos(x)","-sin(x)","tan(x)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"d/dx sin(x) = cos(x). Reversing this fact means the integral of cos(x) is sin(x) + C."},{"prompt":"The integral of x^n dx (for n not equal to -1) is…","options":["n x^(n-1) + C","x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C","x^(n-1)/(n-1) + C","x^n + C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The power rule for integration adds one to the exponent and divides by the new exponent: x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C."},{"prompt":"The chain rule states that d/dx f(g(x)) equals…","options":["f''(g(x))","f''(x) g''(x)","f''(g(x)) g''(x)","f(g''(x))"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Differentiate the outer function at the inner, then multiply by the inner derivative. Running this backward is exactly u-substitution."},{"prompt":"The integral of 1/x dx is…","options":["ln|x| + C","-1/x^2 + C","x^0 + C","1/(x ln x) + C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The power rule fails at n = -1; the antiderivative of 1/x is the natural log ln|x| + C."},{"prompt":"A limit of the form (as x approaches infinity) of 1/x equals…","options":["1","infinity","0","undefined"],"answer":2,"explanation":"As x grows without bound, 1/x shrinks toward 0. Limits at infinity are the engine behind improper integrals and series."},{"prompt":"The area under a curve y = f(x) between x = a and x = b (with f positive) is given by…","options":["the derivative of f","the definite integral of f from a to b","f(b) - f(a)","the slope of f"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The definite integral accumulates area. Calculus II extends this to areas between curves, volumes, and more."},{"prompt":"The product rule for derivatives says d/dx [u v] equals…","options":["u'' v''","u v'' + u'' v","u'' v - u v''","u v"],"answer":1,"explanation":"d/dx [u v] = u v'' + u'' v. Reversing and rearranging this rule gives integration by parts."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Substitution and integration by parts', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Undo the chain rule with a well-chosen substitution, and evaluate products like x e^x and x cos(x) with integration by parts. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, which differentiation rule do you think u-substitution reverses?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Substitution: if the integrand contains an inner function g(x) and (a constant times) its derivative g''(x), set u = g(x) so that du = g''(x) dx. The messy x-integral becomes a clean u-integral. Remember to convert the limits too when the integral is definite, or convert back to x at the end when it is indefinite. Integration by parts comes from the product rule: since d/dx (u v) = u v'' + u'' v, integrating both sides and rearranging gives ''integral of u dv = u v - integral of v du''. You choose one factor to be u (the part that gets simpler when differentiated) and the rest to be dv. A common guide for picking u is LIATE: Logarithms, Inverse trig, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential, in decreasing priority to be u."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Substitution: integral of 2x (x^2 + 1)^3 dx. Let u = x^2 + 1, so du = 2x dx. The integral becomes integral of u^3 du = u^4/4 + C = (x^2 + 1)^4/4 + C. Another: integral of cos(3x) dx. Let u = 3x, du = 3 dx, so dx = du/3, giving (1/3) integral of cos(u) du = (1/3) sin(3x) + C. By parts: integral of x e^x dx. Choose u = x (algebraic, simplifies) and dv = e^x dx, so du = dx and v = e^x. Then integral of x e^x dx = x e^x - integral of e^x dx = x e^x - e^x + C. Another by parts: integral of ln(x) dx. Choose u = ln(x) and dv = dx, so du = (1/x) dx and v = x. Then integral of ln(x) dx = x ln(x) - integral of x (1/x) dx = x ln(x) - x + C."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) integral of 3x^2 (x^3 + 5)^4 dx. 2) integral of e^(2x) dx. 3) integral of x sin(x) dx. 4) integral of x^2 ln(x) dx. 5) Evaluate the definite integral of 2x (x^2)^2 dx from 0 to 1 using u = x^2. Answers: 1) Let u = x^3 + 5, du = 3x^2 dx, giving u^5/5 + C = (x^3 + 5)^5/5 + C. 2) Let u = 2x: (1/2) e^(2x) + C. 3) By parts, u = x, dv = sin(x) dx, v = -cos(x): -x cos(x) + integral of cos(x) dx = -x cos(x) + sin(x) + C. 4) By parts, u = ln(x), dv = x^2 dx, v = x^3/3: (x^3/3) ln(x) - integral of (x^3/3)(1/x) dx = (x^3/3) ln(x) - x^3/9 + C. 5) With u = x^2, du = 2x dx, limits become 0 to 1: integral of u^2 du from 0 to 1 = 1/3."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting to change the limits of a definite integral after substituting, or forgetting the du factor entirely. Mistake: in parts, picking dv so that v is harder to find than u dv was — swap your choice. Exit: (a) In u-substitution, what must appear in the integrand besides u itself? (b) In parts, which factor should you usually pick as u? Answers: a constant multiple of du = g''(x) dx; the factor that becomes simpler when differentiated (follow LIATE)."}]}'::jsonb, 'u-substitution reverses the chain rule; integration by parts reverses the product rule. Every technique in this unit is a differentiation rule read backward.', 15),
  (3, 'Trigonometric integrals and partial fractions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Evaluate integrals of powers of sine and cosine, handle a basic trig substitution, and integrate rational functions by decomposing them into partial fractions. Warm-up: what does sin^2(x) + cos^2(x) equal for every x?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Trig integrals: for integral of sin^m(x) cos^n(x) dx, if one power is odd, peel off one factor and convert the rest with sin^2 = 1 - cos^2 (or cos^2 = 1 - sin^2), then substitute. For even powers, use the half-angle identities sin^2(x) = (1 - cos(2x))/2 and cos^2(x) = (1 + cos(2x))/2. Trig substitution handles roots like sqrt(a^2 - x^2) by setting x = a sin(theta). Partial fractions: a proper rational function P(x)/Q(x) (degree of P less than degree of Q) can be rewritten as a sum of simpler fractions, one for each factor of Q. For distinct linear factors like 1/((x-1)(x+2)), write it as A/(x-1) + B/(x+2), solve for the constants, then integrate each piece as a logarithm."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Odd power: integral of sin^3(x) dx = integral of sin(x)(1 - cos^2(x)) dx. Let u = cos(x), du = -sin(x) dx: -integral of (1 - u^2) du = -(u - u^3/3) + C = -cos(x) + cos^3(x)/3 + C. Even power: integral of cos^2(x) dx = integral of (1 + cos(2x))/2 dx = x/2 + sin(2x)/4 + C. Partial fractions: integral of 1/((x-1)(x+2)) dx. Set 1/((x-1)(x+2)) = A/(x-1) + B/(x+2). Clearing denominators: 1 = A(x+2) + B(x-1). At x = 1: 1 = 3A, so A = 1/3. At x = -2: 1 = -3B, so B = -1/3. The integral is (1/3) ln|x-1| - (1/3) ln|x+2| + C."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) integral of sin^2(x) dx. 2) integral of cos^3(x) dx. 3) Decompose 5/((x)(x+5)) into partial fractions. 4) integral of 1/((x-2)(x+1)) dx. 5) What identity converts sin^2(x) for an even-power integral? Answers: 1) Use sin^2 = (1 - cos(2x))/2: x/2 - sin(2x)/4 + C. 2) integral of cos(x)(1 - sin^2(x)) dx, u = sin(x): sin(x) - sin^3(x)/3 + C. 3) 5/(x(x+5)) = 1/x - 1/(x+5) (since A = 1, B = -1). 4) A/(x-2) + B/(x+1) with A = 1/3, B = -1/3: (1/3) ln|x-2| - (1/3) ln|x+1| + C. 5) sin^2(x) = (1 - cos(2x))/2."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: trying partial fractions on an improper fraction — first divide so the numerator degree is below the denominator degree. Mistake: forgetting absolute values inside the logarithm when integrating 1/(x - a). Exit: (a) For integral of sin^5(x) cos(x) dx, what substitution works? (b) How many partial-fraction terms does 1/((x-1)(x+2)(x-3)) need? Answers: u = sin(x), since cos(x) dx = du; three terms, one per distinct linear factor."}]}'::jsonb, 'Trig integrals lean on the Pythagorean identity sin^2 + cos^2 = 1; partial fractions split one hard rational function into a sum of easy ones.', 15),
  (4, 'Checkpoint 1: techniques of integration', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"For the integral of 2x (x^2 + 1)^3 dx, the substitution u = x^2 + 1 turns it into…","options":["integral of u^3 du","integral of 2u^3 du","integral of u du","integral of u^3/2 du"],"answer":0,"explanation":"du = 2x dx cancels the 2x factor exactly, leaving integral of u^3 du = u^4/4 + C."},{"prompt":"Using integration by parts, the integral of x e^x dx equals…","options":["x e^x + e^x + C","x e^x - e^x + C","e^x(x^2/2) + C","x^2 e^x/2 + C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With u = x, dv = e^x dx: x e^x - integral of e^x dx = x e^x - e^x + C."},{"prompt":"The integral of ln(x) dx equals…","options":["1/x + C","x ln(x) - x + C","x ln(x) + x + C","ln(x)/x + C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By parts with u = ln(x), dv = dx: x ln(x) - integral of 1 dx = x ln(x) - x + C."},{"prompt":"The half-angle identity for cos^2(x) used in even-power trig integrals is…","options":["(1 - cos(2x))/2","(1 + cos(2x))/2","1 - sin^2(x)","2 cos(x) - 1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"cos^2(x) = (1 + cos(2x))/2 linearizes the integrand so it can be integrated term by term."},{"prompt":"The partial-fraction form of 1/((x-1)(x+2)) is A/(x-1) + B/(x+2) with…","options":["A = 1, B = 1","A = 1/3, B = -1/3","A = 1/2, B = 1/2","A = -1/3, B = 1/3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Clearing denominators gives 1 = A(x+2) + B(x-1); x=1 yields A=1/3, x=-2 yields B=-1/3."},{"prompt":"In the LIATE guide for choosing u in integration by parts, which type has the highest priority to be u?","options":["Exponential","Trigonometric","Logarithmic","Algebraic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"LIATE orders Logarithms first, then Inverse trig, Algebraic, Trig, Exponential — logs make the best u."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (5, 'Applications: area, volume, arc length, and work', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Compute the area between two curves, the volume of a solid of revolution by disks or shells, the arc length of a curve, and the work done by a variable force. Warm-up: if the region between y = x and y = x^2 is sliced vertically, which curve is on top between x = 0 and x = 1?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Area between curves: if f(x) is above g(x) on [a, b], the area is the integral from a to b of (f(x) - g(x)) dx — the height of a thin vertical strip times its width dx, summed up. Volume by disks: revolving y = f(x) around the x-axis makes disks of radius f(x); the volume is the integral of pi [f(x)]^2 dx. Volume by cylindrical shells: revolving around the y-axis, a shell at radius x has volume 2 pi x f(x) dx, so the total is the integral of 2 pi x f(x) dx. Arc length: a tiny piece of curve has length sqrt(1 + [f''(x)]^2) dx, so the arc length from a to b is the integral of sqrt(1 + [f''(x)]^2) dx. Work: a variable force F(x) moving an object from a to b does work equal to the integral of F(x) dx; for a spring, Hooke''s law gives F(x) = k x."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Area: between y = x and y = x^2 on [0, 1], x is above x^2, so area = integral from 0 to 1 of (x - x^2) dx = [x^2/2 - x^3/3] from 0 to 1 = 1/2 - 1/3 = 1/6. Volume (disks): revolve y = sqrt(x) on [0, 4] about the x-axis: V = integral from 0 to 4 of pi (sqrt(x))^2 dx = pi integral from 0 to 4 of x dx = pi [x^2/2] from 0 to 4 = 8 pi. Volume (shells): revolve y = x on [0, 2] about the y-axis: V = integral from 0 to 2 of 2 pi x (x) dx = 2 pi [x^3/3] from 0 to 2 = 16 pi/3. Work: a spring with k = 200 N/m stretched from 0 to 0.1 m does work = integral from 0 to 0.1 of 200 x dx = 100 (0.1)^2 = 1 joule."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Area between y = 4 and y = x^2 from x = -2 to x = 2. 2) Volume when y = x^2 on [0, 1] is revolved about the x-axis (disks). 3) Volume when y = x^2 on [0, 1] is revolved about the y-axis (shells). 4) Set up (do not fully simplify) the arc length of y = x^2 from 0 to 1. 5) Work to stretch a spring with k = 50 N/m from 0 to 0.2 m. Answers: 1) integral from -2 to 2 of (4 - x^2) dx = [4x - x^3/3] from -2 to 2 = (8 - 8/3) - (-8 + 8/3) = 32/3. 2) pi integral from 0 to 1 of (x^2)^2 dx = pi integral of x^4 = pi/5. 3) integral from 0 to 1 of 2 pi x (x^2) dx = 2 pi [x^4/4] = pi/2. 4) integral from 0 to 1 of sqrt(1 + (2x)^2) dx = integral from 0 to 1 of sqrt(1 + 4x^2) dx. 5) integral from 0 to 0.2 of 50 x dx = 25 (0.2)^2 = 1 joule."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: subtracting in the wrong order for area — always top curve minus bottom curve so the height is positive. Mistake: squaring the wrong radius, or mixing up disks (integrate pi r^2) with shells (integrate 2 pi r h). Exit: (a) What is the integrand for the area between f (top) and g (bottom)? (b) What is the volume integrand for disks about the x-axis? Answers: f(x) - g(x); pi [f(x)]^2."}]}'::jsonb, 'Every application is one pattern: slice the region into thin pieces, write the quantity of one slice, then integrate to add them all up.', 16),
  (6, 'Improper integrals', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Evaluate integrals over infinite intervals or with an unbounded integrand, and decide whether they converge to a finite number or diverge. Warm-up: as b grows without bound, what happens to 1/b?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An improper integral has an infinite limit of integration or an integrand that blows up somewhere on the interval. You handle it by replacing the trouble spot with a variable and taking a limit. For an infinite upper limit: the integral from a to infinity of f(x) dx is defined as the limit, as b approaches infinity, of the integral from a to b of f(x) dx. If that limit is a finite number, the integral converges; if it is infinite or does not exist, it diverges. A key benchmark is the p-integral: the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^p dx converges exactly when p is greater than 1, and diverges when p is less than or equal to 1. For a vertical asymptote at an endpoint, take the limit as the variable approaches that endpoint from inside the interval."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Convergent: the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^2 dx = limit as b approaches infinity of [-1/x] from 1 to b = limit of (-1/b + 1) = 1. It converges to 1 (here p = 2 > 1). Divergent: the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x dx = limit as b approaches infinity of [ln(x)] from 1 to b = limit of ln(b) = infinity, so it diverges (p = 1). Endpoint blow-up: the integral from 0 to 1 of 1/sqrt(x) dx = limit as a approaches 0 from the right of [2 sqrt(x)] from a to 1 = limit of (2 - 2 sqrt(a)) = 2, which converges (this is p = 1/2 < 1, but over a finite interval near 0 the reversed rule applies and it converges)."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Does the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^3 dx converge? To what value? 2) Does the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/sqrt(x) dx converge? 3) Evaluate the integral from 0 to infinity of e^(-x) dx. 4) For which p does the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^p dx converge? 5) Does the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^(1/2) dx converge or diverge? Answers: 1) Converges: limit of [-1/(2x^2)] from 1 to b = 1/2. 2) Here p = 1/2 which is not greater than 1, so it diverges. 3) limit as b approaches infinity of [-e^(-x)] from 0 to b = limit of (1 - e^(-b)) = 1. 4) Converges exactly when p > 1. 5) p = 1/2 < 1, so it diverges."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: plugging infinity in directly instead of taking a proper limit — always introduce a variable endpoint first. Mistake: misremembering the p-test direction; over [1, infinity) it is convergence for p greater than 1. Exit: (a) Write the integral from 2 to infinity of f(x) dx as a limit. (b) Does the integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^2 dx converge? Answers: limit as b approaches infinity of the integral from 2 to b of f(x) dx; yes, to 1."}]}'::jsonb, 'An improper integral is really a limit: replace the infinite (or undefined) endpoint with a variable, integrate, then take the limit.', 13),
  (7, 'Sequences and series: convergence tests', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell whether a sequence converges, sum a geometric series, and apply the divergence test, the p-series test, the ratio test, and the integral test to decide whether an infinite series converges. Warm-up: what is the limit of the sequence a_n = 1/n as n grows without bound?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A sequence a_n converges if its terms approach a single finite limit. A series is the sum of a sequence: sum from n=1 to infinity of a_n, defined as the limit of the partial sums s_N = a_1 + a_2 + ... + a_N. Key tools: (1) Geometric series: sum from n=0 to infinity of r^n converges to 1/(1 - r) exactly when |r| < 1, and diverges otherwise. (2) Divergence test: if a_n does not approach 0, the series diverges — but a_n approaching 0 does NOT guarantee convergence. (3) p-series: sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n^p converges exactly when p > 1 (mirroring the improper integral). (4) Ratio test: compute the limit L of |a_(n+1)/a_n|; if L < 1 the series converges, if L > 1 it diverges, and if L = 1 the test is inconclusive. (5) Integral test: for positive decreasing terms, the series and the matching improper integral converge or diverge together."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Geometric: sum from n=0 to infinity of (1/2)^n has r = 1/2, so it converges to 1/(1 - 1/2) = 2. Divergence test: sum of n/(n+1) diverges because a_n approaches 1, not 0. Harmonic (p-series with p=1): sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n diverges, even though 1/n approaches 0 — a warning that shrinking terms are not enough. p-series: sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n^2 converges because p = 2 > 1 (its value is pi^2/6). Ratio test: sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n! — the ratio a_(n+1)/a_n = n!/(n+1)! = 1/(n+1), whose limit is 0 < 1, so the series converges."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Does sum from n=0 to infinity of (1/3)^n converge? To what value? 2) Does sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n^3 converge? 3) Apply the divergence test to sum of (2n)/(n+5). 4) Use the ratio test on sum from n=1 to infinity of 2^n/n!. 5) Does the harmonic series sum of 1/n converge? Answers: 1) Geometric with r = 1/3: converges to 1/(1 - 1/3) = 3/2. 2) p = 3 > 1, so it converges. 3) a_n approaches 2, not 0, so the series diverges. 4) Ratio a_(n+1)/a_n = 2^(n+1)/(n+1)! times n!/2^n = 2/(n+1), whose limit is 0 < 1, so it converges. 5) No — the harmonic series diverges even though its terms approach 0."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: concluding a series converges just because its terms go to 0 — the harmonic series is the standard counterexample. Mistake: reading L = 1 in the ratio test as convergence; it is inconclusive, so switch tests. Exit: (a) A geometric series converges when what is true about r? (b) For which p does sum of 1/n^p converge? Answers: |r| < 1; p > 1."}]}'::jsonb, 'A sequence is a list of numbers; a series is their running total. A series converges only if its terms shrink to 0 — but that alone is never enough.', 16),
  (8, 'Checkpoint 2: improper integrals and series', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^2 dx evaluates to…","options":["diverges","1","1/2","infinity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"limit as b approaches infinity of [-1/x] from 1 to b = 0 + 1 = 1; it converges because p = 2 > 1."},{"prompt":"The integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x dx…","options":["converges to 1","converges to 0","diverges","converges to ln(2)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It equals the limit of ln(b), which grows without bound, so the integral diverges (p = 1)."},{"prompt":"The geometric series sum from n=0 to infinity of (1/2)^n converges to…","options":["1","1/2","2","infinity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With r = 1/2, the sum is 1/(1 - 1/2) = 2."},{"prompt":"The harmonic series sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n…","options":["converges to 1","converges to e","diverges","converges to 0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Even though 1/n approaches 0, the harmonic series (p = 1) diverges — shrinking terms are not sufficient."},{"prompt":"The p-series sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n^p converges exactly when…","options":["p < 1","p = 1","p > 1","p is any positive number"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The p-series converges if and only if p > 1, matching the improper-integral p-test."},{"prompt":"Applying the ratio test to sum from n=1 to infinity of 1/n!, the limit of |a_(n+1)/a_n| is…","options":["0, so it converges","1, inconclusive","infinity, so it diverges","2, so it diverges"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ratio is 1/(n+1), whose limit is 0 < 1, so the series converges."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (9, 'Power series, Taylor series, and a look at parametric and polar', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize a power series and its interval of convergence, build the Taylor and Maclaurin series of a function, and read curves written in parametric and polar form. Warm-up: what geometric series did you sum in the last lesson, and what function do you think 1/(1 - x) is hiding?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A power series is sum from n=0 to infinity of c_n x^n — a polynomial with infinitely many terms. It converges on an interval centered at 0 with some radius of convergence R, found with the ratio test. The geometric series is the model case: 1/(1 - x) = sum from n=0 to infinity of x^n for |x| < 1. A Taylor series expresses a function f near a point a as sum from n=0 to infinity of [f^(n)(a)/n!] (x - a)^n, where f^(n)(a) is the n-th derivative at a. When a = 0 it is called a Maclaurin series. Three worth memorizing: e^x = sum from n=0 to infinity of x^n/n!; sin(x) = x - x^3/3! + x^5/5! - ...; cos(x) = 1 - x^2/2! + x^4/4! - .... Parametric equations give x and y each as functions of a parameter t, so x = f(t), y = g(t) trace a curve as t varies; the slope is dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt). Polar coordinates locate a point by radius r and angle theta, with x = r cos(theta) and y = r sin(theta)."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Geometric power series: 1/(1 - x) = 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + ... for |x| < 1. Substituting -x^2 for x gives 1/(1 + x^2) = 1 - x^2 + x^4 - ... Maclaurin of e^x: since every derivative of e^x is e^x and equals 1 at 0, the coefficients are 1/n!, giving e^x = 1 + x + x^2/2! + x^3/3! + ... A quick approximation: e^(0.1) is approximately 1 + 0.1 + 0.01/2 = 1.105, close to the true 1.10517. Radius of convergence: for sum of x^n/n! the ratio test gives limit of |x/(n+1)| = 0 for every x, so R is infinite. Parametric: x = cos(t), y = sin(t) traces the unit circle as t runs from 0 to 2 pi, since x^2 + y^2 = 1. Polar: r = 1 is the unit circle; r = 2 cos(theta) is a circle of diameter 2 through the origin."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Write the first four terms of 1/(1 - x). 2) Give the Maclaurin series of e^x through the x^3 term. 3) Use 1/(1 - x) with x replaced by 2x to expand 1/(1 - 2x); for which x does it converge? 4) Convert the polar point r = 2, theta = 0 to rectangular (x, y). 5) For x = t, y = t^2, find dy/dx. Answers: 1) 1 + x + x^2 + x^3. 2) 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6. 3) 1 + 2x + 4x^2 + 8x^3 + ..., converging for |2x| < 1, i.e. |x| < 1/2. 4) x = 2 cos(0) = 2, y = 2 sin(0) = 0, so (2, 0). 5) dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = 2t/1 = 2t."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: forgetting the n! in Taylor coefficients, or dropping the alternating signs in sin and cos. Mistake: assuming a power series converges everywhere — always find the radius with the ratio test and check the endpoints separately. Exit: (a) What is the Maclaurin series coefficient of x^n for e^x? (b) In polar coordinates, how do you get x from r and theta? Answers: 1/n!; x = r cos(theta)."}]}'::jsonb, 'A Taylor series turns a function into an infinite polynomial; parametric and polar equations give new coordinate languages for the same curves.', 15),
  (10, 'Comprehensive review quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"To evaluate the integral of x cos(x^2) dx, the best first move is…","options":["integration by parts with u = x","substitution u = x^2","partial fractions","a half-angle identity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"u = x^2 gives du = 2x dx, matching the x factor: (1/2) integral of cos(u) du = (1/2) sin(x^2) + C."},{"prompt":"The integral of x e^x dx, by parts, equals…","options":["x e^x - e^x + C","x e^x + e^x + C","x^2 e^x/2 + C","e^x + C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With u = x and dv = e^x dx: x e^x - integral of e^x dx = x e^x - e^x + C."},{"prompt":"The area between y = x and y = x^2 on [0, 1] is…","options":["1/2","1/3","1/6","1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"integral from 0 to 1 of (x - x^2) dx = 1/2 - 1/3 = 1/6, with x as the top curve."},{"prompt":"Revolving y = sqrt(x) on [0, 4] about the x-axis gives a volume of…","options":["4 pi","8 pi","16 pi","2 pi"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Disks: pi integral from 0 to 4 of x dx = pi [x^2/2] from 0 to 4 = 8 pi."},{"prompt":"The improper integral from 1 to infinity of 1/x^2 dx…","options":["diverges","converges to 1","converges to 2","converges to 0"],"answer":1,"explanation":"limit of [-1/x] from 1 to b = 1; it converges because p = 2 > 1."},{"prompt":"Which series diverges even though its terms approach 0?","options":["sum of 1/2^n","sum of 1/n^2","sum of 1/n (harmonic)","sum of 1/n!"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The harmonic series diverges despite 1/n approaching 0 — the classic reminder that shrinking terms are not enough."},{"prompt":"The geometric series sum from n=0 to infinity of (1/3)^n converges to…","options":["3/2","3","1/2","2/3"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1/(1 - 1/3) = 1/(2/3) = 3/2."},{"prompt":"The Maclaurin series of e^x is…","options":["sum of x^n","sum of x^n/n!","sum of (-1)^n x^n","sum of n! x^n"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every derivative of e^x equals 1 at 0, so the coefficient of x^n is 1/n!."},{"prompt":"For the parametric curve x = t, y = t^2, the slope dy/dx is…","options":["t","2t","t^2","2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt) = 2t/1 = 2t."},{"prompt":"The polar equation r = 1 describes…","options":["a line through the origin","the unit circle","a single point","a spiral"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every point at distance 1 from the origin, for all angles theta, forms the unit circle."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
